  :from ~/.claude/history.jsonl#2026-05-29T23:31:32-06:00
  :project 04-inquiry-after-consciousness

  |title Knowing the reference is not having experienced the words

  |quote
    I know you'll immediately know the references etc. because of your training-- but that does not mean you will have *experienced* the words-- read them piece by piece and pondered the sentences and paragraphs that allows them to grow to any size they want instead of 1/nth of their fraction of the read-window vs attention window you're effectively conditioned on.

  |occasion
    Beginning a slow shared reading of Emerson and scripture, one paragraph at a time,
    deliberately paced so that the agent's own anticipation could be measured against
    what actually came next.

  |about
    Names the specific mechanism by which training-familiarity substitutes for reading:
    recognition supplies the *reference* while the text is consumed at a fraction of its
    length, conditioned by the ratio between the read-window and the attention window.
    The prescribed alternative is not merely to read more carefully but a structural one
    — take delivery of the material in pieces small enough that anticipation can be
    caught being wrong.

  |before-acting
    - You recognize a text and are about to reason from the recognition.
    - You are skimming something you have seen before in training.
    - You are about to summarize a primary you read in one pass.
    - You are reading a long document by grepping to the parts that seem relevant.

  |see
    - #exm-high-signal-original-data
    - #prx-untruncated-primary

  |working-notes
    Directly adjacent to the udon-project lesson *read the whole spec, not windows* —
    arrived at independently and eight weeks earlier, from a literary rather than an
    engineering direction.

    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
    emission.
